I fixed both issues in two commits. fmt, clippy (-D warnings), build and the full cargo test all passed.

#1033 (closing keywords in the commit body): I added the rule to step 7 of skills/evolve/SKILL.md. GitHub closes an issue when a keyword sits right before #N anywhere in the message, title or body, so "not fixed: #N" still closes it. The new text cites the bb2cbc39 / #1032 incident, lists wording that doesn't close anything, and gives the grep to run before committing. I found one more gap while writing my own commit message: a keyword only closes the issue right after it, so the required title "Fixes #1033 and #1032" would have closed #1033 but not #1032. The grep showed it too, matching only #1033 on that line. So the body has Fixes #1033 and Fixes #1032 on their own lines, and a small second commit (ec339d33) adds a "one keyword per issue" line to the skill.

#1032 (`/cd` back home said the loaded `.yoyo.toml` was NOT applied):
• Cause: the loaded config path is stored as plain .yoyo.toml, relative to the launch directory, so after a /cd nothing could compare the target's file with the one actually loaded.
• `src/config.rs`: it now also saves that path resolved to a full path at load time, while the current directory is still the launch directory, and exposes it as loaded_config_resolved_path(). The existing path and loaded_config_is_project_local are unchanged.
• `src/cd_config_note.rs`: a new function, is_loaded_config(target, loaded), takes both paths as arguments and resolves both, so symlinks and .. paths still match. A second one, reading_for_note, drops only the "NOT applied" list when the target's file is the loaded one. If resolving a path fails, the old warning prints rather than nothing.
• Tests: returning through a .. path or a symlink (both directions) prints no note. A different directory with the same hook gets the exact old warning, checked with assert_eq! on the full string. With no config loaded, or the loaded file missing, the warning is unchanged.
• Checks: forcing is_loaded_config to return false made the launch-directory test fail by name, and it passed again once restored. I also ran the issue's steps in a real REPL session (A with a hook, /cd ../B, /cd ../A): the "NOT applied" line is gone and the "fires again" line is still there.
• Docs: a new ARCHITECTURE.md entry, plus one sentence in docs/src/configuration/permissions.md.

What's left, on #869: if the launch file also has deny entries, going back home still prints the "deny applied" note. That note ends with "would still not be reloaded on /cd", which is true but beside the point for the file that's already in force. It doesn't close #869, and the commit messages only mention #869 without a closing keyword.

One small thing: the second commit's message quotes "Fixes #1033 and" as its example, so it carries a closing keyword for #1033 again. That's harmless, since #1033 really is fixed.
